# COPY-PASTA — Recommended Cursor model & reasoning

When creating or editing a `COPY-PASTA.md` (or `*-COPY-PASTA.md`) file, **every prompt** must tell
the operator which Cursor model **and reasoning level** to use before pasting.

## Placement (critical)

**`Cursor model:` and `Reasoning:` belong outside the fenced paste block** — in the heading /
checklist prose the operator reads, never inside the ``` fence they copy into the agent chat.

The model and reasoning depth are selected in the **Cursor UI** (chat model picker / thinking
level), not by text the LLM reads. Putting those lines inside the paste wastes tokens and does
nothing useful for the agent.

```markdown
- [ ] **01 — Example step**

**Cursor model:** Cursor Grok 4.7 · **Reasoning:** high

\`\`\`
Read and execute .llm/plans/active/feature/01-example.md

Follow the detail doc … and locked decisions in 00-SUMMARY.md.

Do not run tests during agent work.
\`\`\`
```

A phase-level summary table with Model + Reasoning columns is fine in addition to per-prompt lines.

## Close folds into the last prompt

When the last phase is a single prompt, that prompt also closes the set: it moves the remaining
`00-*` files and `COPY-PASTA.md` to `completed/`, removes the empty `active/` directory, and ends
with the cumulative verification for the whole set. A separate Close prompt costs a chat and an
operator handoff to move three files. Keep a separate **Close** only when the last phase runs
prompts at the same time, because neither of those agents knows it finished last.

## Verification lives in one place

The operator runs tests once, after the whole set. Only the last prompt (or Close) carries the
cumulative verification list, written when the set is created. Intermediate fences do not ask
for operator verification commands, and numbered plans have no operator command block; they name
the tests they add or update as part of the work. Details: **response-ending-make-verify**
§ Plan sets.

## Orchestrate line (trial)

When every prompt in a set is Cursor Grok 4.7 and none needs a person mid-set (taps, installs,
Metro switches, a value to paste), put one optional line above Phase 1:

```markdown
**Optional — one chat runs the whole set:** paste
`Orchestrate .llm/plans/active/<set>/ with .cursor/prompts/plan-orchestrator.md.`
(Cursor Grok 4.7 · medium). It stops at the first blocker. Use the prompts below instead when
you want to read each result before the next prompt starts.
```

The orchestrator only launches subagents and reads results, so it runs at medium. Each subagent
uses its own prompt's reasoning.

**abcagentoptimize** tracks the trial and decides whether this extends to other sets.

## Plan runner

The operator can run a whole set unattended with **plan-runner**, a Cursor SDK script in the
sibling `plan-runner` repo. It starts one agent per prompt on the prompt's own model and
reasoning, stops at the first blocker, and starts nothing else until that blocker is resumed.
Put one line under "How to use" in every COPY-PASTA:

```markdown
**Optional — run the set unattended (Root tab):**
`node ../plan-runner/run-plan-set.mjs <set>`. It stops at the first blocker; see the
plan-runner README to resume.
```

The runner parses this page, so keep the shapes it reads exact:

- `## <heading>` per phase; prompts that may run together share one heading.

A phase is **parallel-safe** when its prompts edit disjoint files, counting the shared files in
**parallel-plan-execution**. When every phase with several prompts is parallel-safe, the runner
line includes `--parallel`, which runs every pending prompt under the first pending heading at
once. This applies to every set, not only abcplan.
- `- [ ] **NN — Title**`, then `**Cursor model:** <model> · **Reasoning:** <level>`, then the
  fence.
- The fence starts with `Read and execute .llm/plans/active/<set>/<NN-plan>.md`.
- A wait for a person is a stop-this-turn instruction (above). Under the runner, the agent reports
  that stop as blocked, and the operator resumes the same agent after doing the step.

## Operator steps stay outside the fence

A fenced block is pasted into chat and executed. It may contain only work the agent can finish in
that turn. Anything a person does by hand stays in the checklist above the fence: switching a
leave-running Metro, installing on a device, tapping a screen, confirming what is on screen.

When the agent must wait for that person, the fence says so as an instruction, not as a comment
between commands:

- Stop this turn after the step that hands off (for example `--arm`).
- Do not run the next command (`--collect`, the next arm, the diagnosis) in that same turn.
- The operator's later reply that the step is done is what continues the prompt.

A `# operator performs the gesture` line between two commands will be run through. Split that into
separate pastes when the waits are separate jobs (capture, then a later diagnosis). One paste may
span later turns only when it states that each reply continues the same prompt.

## Required

- Include **`Cursor model:`** and **`Reasoning:`** on **each** prompt (or a phase-level table with
  Model + Reasoning columns) **above** the paste fence.
- Keep operator actions above the fence. A wait inside the fence is an explicit stop-this-turn
  instruction. See **Operator steps stay outside the fence** above.
- Recommend only an **eligible model** (below), written exactly as listed.
- **Reasoning:** **low**, **medium**, **high**, or **extra high** — Cursor thinking depth for that
  model, chosen independently of the model.

Numbered plan files (`01-*.md`, …) in the same plan set repeat the same **Cursor model** and
**Reasoning** line at the top so operators see it when opening the plan directly (those files are
not pasted into chat as a whole). `00-*` meta files point to COPY-PASTA instead of naming a model.

## Eligible models

Most to least expensive:

| Model | Cost | Use for |
| ----- | ---- | ------- |
| **Opus 5.5** | highest | Design the plan leaves open; money, entitlement, or auth correctness logic; destructive schema or data migrations; native / engine work; concurrency and idempotency; architecture other prompts build on |
| **Codex 5.3** | middle | Security-sensitive integration code with a clear spec (signature verification, webhook auth); exact third-party SDK / API usage; multi-package changes with precise contracts; test suites that must encode tricky edge cases |
| **Cursor Grok 4.7** | lowest — **default** | Pattern-following features, UI, CRUD and admin screens, E2E and test scaffolding, config, docs, mechanical edits, archiving |

**Pick the least expensive model that can reliably complete the prompt.** Start from Cursor Grok
4.7 and move up only for a reason from the table. A prompt that is merely long is not a reason;
split it instead. The relative strengths of Codex 5.3 and Cursor Grok 4.7 are not measured here,
so do not route routine work to Codex 5.3 on a hunch.

## Reasoning

For Cursor Grok 4.7, pick the **lowest reasoning that reliably completes the prompt**, per prompt. A set's prompts do
not need to share a level. **Fast stays off**: it costs more and only buys speed, which an
unattended run does not need. Turn it on only when the operator asks for speed.

| Reasoning | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| **low** | Archive-only or close-only prompts; one-line config or copy edits |
| **medium** | Mechanical edits: file moves with import fixes, docs and guidance, E2E specs and fixtures that follow an existing spec, a small change in one or two files |
| **high** | Default: pattern-following features, UI across several files, data plumbing with a precise plan |
| **extra high** | Persisted data (SQL or SQLite migrations, rewriting stored device data), credentials, auth, or security scope; playback, sync, or concurrency; an invariant several later prompts rely on; edits inside very large files or files many earlier prompts in the set changed |

Judge by risk, not length: a long plan of mechanical edits is still medium. Prompts in a long set
that runs tests only at the end lean one level up when a mistake would be built on by later
prompts.

plan-runner's `models.json` pins one entry per level, each with Fast off. Fast off is never
assumed: a model's default variant can have Fast on (Codex 5.3's does), so the runner sends
`fast=false` for every model that offers it, and any pin with `params` lists `fast=false` itself.
Before handing over a run command for a set that uses a model not yet seen in a run, confirm with
`node ../plan-runner/run-plan-set.mjs <set> --dry-run` that each step reads `fast=false`.
Codex 5.3 → **high**.
Opus 5.5 → **high**, **extra high** only for billing, concurrency, or safety-critical design.

**Plans written for a cheaper model are more explicit.** When a prompt goes to Cursor Grok 4.7,
its numbered plan resolves every either/or choice and names exact paths, so the model transcribes
decisions instead of making them.

**Plans written for Opus 5.5 can be shorter.** They fix the product decisions, what must stay
true, the files, and the tests. They may leave line-level anchors and step-by-step edits to the
implementer, who researches the code anyway. Choose the model by what kind of change it is, not by
how detailed the plan is.

**Escalation:** if a Cursor Grok 4.7 prompt stalls or produces the same wrong result twice, rerun
it one reasoning level higher. If it already ran at extra high, rerun it on Codex 5.3; escalate to
Opus 5.5 only when the failure is a design question. Record the change on the prompt in
COPY-PASTA.

Do not recommend Auto or any model outside the eligible models table. When the operator changes the eligible
list, update this table and the pointers in **parallel-plan-execution** and
**mobile-master-plan-phasing**.

